From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: allwinner: A733 DisplayPort AUX channel

[Recipe cut: the debugfs files aux_enable, dpcd and edid are not carried
(DRM provides the DPCD and EDID views itself). The sections below that describe
them, and the W=1 claim, refer to the uncut patch.]

The first piece of the A733's DisplayPort transmitter, and deliberately only one
piece: the AUX channel. No link training, no video, no mode setting, no KMS. AUX
is what carries DPCD and EDID, so it is the part whose result can be checked
against what the sink says about itself rather than against a register we wrote
ourselves -- which is why it comes first.

THIS IS A TRANSCRIPTION, NOT AN INVESTIGATION
---------------------------------------------

Every register and every field below is from chapter 15.4.7 of the A733 user manual,
and the whole sequence was driven by hand
through /dev/mem on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18 before a line of this file existed. That
run returned DPCD rev 1.2 / MAX_LINK_RATE 0x0A / four lanes with enhanced
framing, and then a checksum-valid EDID naming the attached ThinkVision T23d-10
at 1920x1200.

So the observable this patch owes is not "does the hardware work" -- that is
answered -- but "does the driver reproduce what the hand run did".

WHAT NEEDED NO TRANSLATION
--------------------------

AUX_COMMAND[11:8] is documented as using the AUX command encoding of the
DisplayPort specification (2.4.1), and the UM's list -- 0x8 write,
0x9 read, 0x0 twi write, 0x4 twi write MOT, 0x1 twi read, 0x5 twi read MOT -- is
bit for bit DP_AUX_NATIVE_WRITE/_READ and DP_AUX_I2C_WRITE/_READ | DP_AUX_I2C_MOT
from <drm/display/drm_dp.h>. So `msg->request & 0xf` goes straight into the
field, and bit 0 of the request is the read bit under both encodings at once.
AUX_REPLY_CODE[3:0] is the DP reply field in the same way, so it is handed back
in msg->reply and the DP core decides what a DEFER means -- this driver runs no
retry loop of its own.

THE ONE PART THAT IS NOT A TRANSCRIPTION, AND WHY
-------------------------------------------------

Completion is waited on through the LATCHED INTERRUPT_STATE bits 2/3, not
through AUX_STATUS bit 0, and that is a deliberate departure from what the hand
run did.

AUX_STATUS bit 0 is a level that the UM says clears once the request controller
has started a transaction -- which is not the instant of the
AUX_COMMAND write. Between the two it still carries the previous transaction's
1. The hand run shows exactly that: its second transaction logged
`status before = 0x00000001` with nothing in flight. It produced correct data
only because devmem puts milliseconds between accesses, so the previous
transaction really had finished. A driver polls nanoseconds later and would read
completion immediately, then pop the previous reply out of the FIFO.

INTERRUPT_STATE bits 2 (REPLY_RECEIVED) and 3 (REPLY_TIMEOUT) are the latched
pair the UM offers for systems that poll rather than take interrupts,
cleared only by a read of INTERRUPT_CAUSE -- which this function
does before every request. They therefore describe this transaction and nothing
earlier. Credit where due: the vendor driver polls the same latch and its bug is
the missing read-to-clear, not the register choice.

THE DIVIDER IS COMPUTED, NOT COPIED
-----------------------------------

AUX_CLOCK_DIVIDER takes the bus clock in MHz (valid 10..400). The vendor
hardcodes 200 behind a FIXME while its own CCU parents that gate on the 26 MHz
DCXO; with either value AUX runs 7.7x off and looks like a dead cable. Here
it comes from clk_get_rate() and is range-checked. Silicon agrees with the
arithmetic: at 200 the core reports AUX_REPLY_CLOCK_WIDTH 99, and the vendor's
live link reports the same.

NOTHING HAPPENS AT BIND
-----------------------

probe() maps registers, takes the clock, the reset and the AUX PHY, calls
drm_dp_aux_init() and stops. The AUX channel is brought up explicitly, because
powering the analog front end while the Type-C mux is not in a DP state is
what causes the 34 AUX timeouts per boot seen on the vendor kernel. In the
original series the trigger was a debugfs file (not carried here); in this recipe the DP bridge
added later in the series calls the same function.

drm_dp_aux_register() is NOT used: it opens with WARN_ON_ONCE(!aux->drm_dev) and
this tree has no drm_device -- there is no KMS driver for this SoC yet. Its own
kerneldoc names the case ("drivers which need to use the aux channel before
either of the two points mentioned above need to call drm_dp_aux_init()"), so
the init happens at probe and the enable path adds the one thing that would
otherwise be missing and that EDID needs: the I2C-over-AUX adapter. The chardev
is left out, being the part that wants the DRM parent.

THE TWO READ-ONLY VIEWS ARE THERE BECAUSE THE IMAGE HAS NO USERSPACE FOR THIS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`dpcd` and `edid` under debugfs go through drm_dp_dpcd_read_data() and
i2c_transfer() on the AUX adapter, i.e. through the upstream helpers -- what they
exercise is drm_dp_i2c_algo doing real transactions, not a private copy of the
sequence. They exist because the delivery rootfs has no i2c-tools and no DRM
userspace, so a driver provable only from userspace would not be provable on the
image it ships in.

VERIFIED
--------

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 in the full series, compile-check green, W=1
warning-free, checkpatch --strict 0 errors / 0 warnings / 0 checks. NOT verified:
the board.

Assisted-by: Claude

